;;; A positive derived under `(absent (q one))` survives into a world that
;;; commits `(q one)` — M1e2 **W8**, contested until *forbid* refused the
;;; program (below). Register row `W8/stale-naf-fork`
;;; (`corpus/wrong_answers.toml`), fixed by the refusal since M1e2 S1e2.2.5.
;;;
;;; ── The program ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; `(p one)` is derived at root because `(q one)` is absent. `p` and `q`
;;; together are a contradiction. The goal is `q`, so the rung proposes
;;; `(q one)`.
;;;
;;; ── What the engine said, until it refused ─────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; The walk, as banked:
;;;
;;;   Solution k = 1, `{(p one)}` plus `(not (q one))`, `exhausted = true`
;;;
;;; Committing `(q one)` falsifies `derive-p`'s guard, but nothing retracts
;;; (`absent_semantics.md`: *retraction / truth maintenance: not provided*), so
;;; the fork held the stale `(p one)` beside `(q one)`, the clash fired, and
;;; the lookahead killed the candidate: the state `{(q one)}` was unreachable.
;;; R said the same once the walk was gone (S1e2.2.5's re-take, banked in
;;; `c887a35b` on 2026-11-04): one state, ∅, root holding `(p one)`, and the
;;; fork `{(q one)}` dead, the one of the cube's two nodes holding a stale
;;; fact.
;;;
;;; ── The answer, by hand: a reading ─────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; Q-M1e2.2 — does a guard's `absent` premise bind the state the search ends
;;; in?
;;;
;;;   guards are premises   committing `(q one)` withdraws `(p one)`, the
;;;                         clash never fires, `{(q one)}` is alive, and root
;;;                         is not maximal: **`Solution`, k = 1, `{(q one)}`**.
;;;   append-only           the answer above was right: the fork with both is
;;;                         dead, and root is the solution.
;;;
;;; The program is stratified (`q` is no rule's conclusion), so the third
;;; reading — refuse what cannot be stratified — does not reach it.
;;;
;;; Banked from M1e2's probes at S1e2.1.1.
;;;
;;; ── 2026-11-04 — refused, *forbid*'s *via* (M1e2 S1e2.2.5) ────────────────
;;;
;;; *Forbid* (Q-M1e2.5, the user's of 2026-11-02): a refutation may not rest
;;; on an `absent` over a relation a commitment can still extend, because a
;;; state that has not yet decided it would be called dead, and a commitment
;;; would lift that. The user ruled its F2 *via, per activator*: a refutation
;;; resting on an `absent` only through a fact a rule derived under it
;;; counts. Here `p-and-q-clash` reads `(p ?x)`, which `derive-p` derives under
;;; `(absent (q ?x))`, and `q` is a relation a commitment can extend; unfolded
;;; it is `(thing ?x) (absent (q ?x)) (q ?x) ⟹ (false)`. So the program is
;;; refused at load, every question about a commitment exits 1 naming
;;; `p-and-q-clash`, `derive-p` and `q`, and register row W8/stale-naf-fork
;;; left F21's deferral, fixed with the refusal as its right answer. The two
;;; readings above are what Q-M1e2.2 (F21's D2) asks of a program that loads;
;;; this one no longer does, and `W8/features-07` keeps the question.
;;;
;;; Re-taken at M1e2 S1e2.2.6 (2026-11-04): `ein solve` in every mode,
;;; `ein oracle` and `ein render lattice` exit 1 with that one line on
;;; stderr. `ein test` exits 2: here because no `:expect` is stated
;;; (*nothing to check*), and on a copy stating one because the file does not
;;; load. `ein saturate` still saturates, deriving `(p one)`, and the views
;;; that take no universe (`render rules`, `constraints`, `program`, `kb`)
;;; still draw.
(relation thing T :why "{?1} is a thing")
(relation p T :why "p holds of {?1}")
(relation q T :why "q holds of {?1}")
(thing one)
(rule derive-p ()
  :match  (and (thing ?x) (absent (q ?x)))
  :assert (p ?x) :why "{?x} is a thing and no q fact holds of it, so p holds of {?x}")
(rule p-and-q-clash ()
  :match  (and (p ?x) (q ?x))
  :assert (false) :why "both p and q hold of {?x}, which cannot be")
(query :goal (q ?x))
